---
# HOW MANY BYTES A PAGE HANDS THE VISITOR.
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE FAILURE THIS EXISTS FOR
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# On 2026-09-04 `/uk/london/movies` was taking 7-22 SECONDS to load over a normal connection, and
# every dashboard said the web tier was healthy. Both readings were correct: the server produced
# the response header in ~50 ms and then handed the visitor 1.27 MB of gzipped HTML. Latency
# metrics stop at the response header -- deliberately, because the SSE streams would otherwise put
# a multi-minute tail in the histogram -- so THE ENTIRE COST WAS INVISIBLE. It was found by
# somebody curling the page by hand.
#
# It is not one page and it is not the worst one. Measured the same day, gzipped:
#
#   /us/los-angeles/movies  1.66 MB   70,209 showtimes   <- worst
#   /us/new-york/movies     1.31 MB   50,739 showtimes
#   /uk/london/movies       1.27 MB   28,710 showtimes
#   /us/chicago/movies      1.17 MB   42,780 showtimes
#   /poznan/                0.21 MB    2,224 showtimes   <- typical
#
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A SIZE RULE AND NOT A LATENCY ONE
# ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# web-latency.rules cannot see this and no tuning of it could. It reads
# `kinowo_web_http_request_duration_seconds`, which by construction measures time-to-header; on
# these exact pages that is ~50 ms. A size histogram is a different question about the same
# request, and it is the one a visitor on a train is actually asking.
#
# THE PAGE HAS NO UPPER BOUND BY CONSTRUCTION. The listing renders the whole city repertoire --
# every film, every cinema, every showtime, each with its booking link -- so weight tracks the
# market. Los Angeles is 33x Poznan's showtime count today, and a new metro is onboarded by adding
# a row to a roster. That is why this alerts on an ABSOLUTE size rather than on growth: there is no
# baseline to grow from, only the size the corpus happens to be.
groups:
  - name: web-page-weight
    rules:
      - alert: WebPageTooHeavy
        # p95 rather than max: one enormous response is a curiosity, but a 95th percentile past the
        # threshold means it is what people are routinely being handed.
        expr: >-
          (
            histogram_quantile(0.95,
              sum by (le, country, route) (
                rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_bucket{
                  route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|/api/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks|sitemap).*"
                }[30m])
              )
            ) > 1048576
          )
          and on (country, route)
          (
            sum by (country, route) (
              rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_count[30m])
            ) >= 0.05
          )
        # An hour, because this is a property of the corpus rather than an event. It moves when a
        # city's repertoire moves -- the weekly programme drop, a new market -- and none of those
        # want paging within minutes.
        for: 1h
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} {{ $labels.route }} is serving {{ $value | humanize1024 }}B pages"
          description: >-
            This route's 95th-percentile response is over a megabyte on the wire, AFTER gzip. The
            server is probably answering fast -- check web-latency.rules, which will read healthy --
            because the cost is in the transfer, not the render. The cause is almost always the
            listing rendering an entire city's repertoire in one document: measure it with
            `curl -H 'Accept-Encoding: gzip' -o /dev/null -w '%{size_download}'` and count
            `data-time="` occurrences for the showtime total. The fix is to bound what is rendered,
            not to compress harder -- gzip is already on, and the repeated markup compresses well
            enough that trimming attributes measured 1.4% while rendering 60 films instead of 784
            measured 80%.

      # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      # AND SEPARATELY: DID IT SUDDENLY CHANGE?
      # ------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
      #
      # `WebPageTooHeavy` above answers "is this page big". It cannot answer "did this page just
      # become a different size", and on 2026-09-06 that was the question. A deploy taught the
      # origin to answer brotli; Cloudflare caches ONE variant per URL and could not re-compress it
      # under `no-transform`, so it DECOMPRESSED and handed gzip-only clients the raw body:
      #
      #   /uk/manchester/                297,089 B  ->  3,789,572 B
      #   /uk/:city/api/repertoire       ~200,000 B ->  1,470,154 B    <- the Android app's fetch
      #
      # Nothing alerted. `WebPageTooHeavy` could not: `/api/` is excluded from its selector, and on
      # the page routes the blow-up hit only the gzip-only slice of traffic, which is too small to
      # move a 95th percentile. It was found by hand, again.
      #
      # SO THIS ONE USES THE MEAN, NOT A PERCENTILE. A regression that hits a MINORITY of requests
      # moves the mean in proportion to that minority and leaves p95 alone; that is exactly the
      # shape above, and it is the shape of any change that depends on what the client sent.
      #
      # AND IT COMPARES AGAINST YESTERDAY rather than a constant, because unlike absolute weight
      # there IS a baseline for "the same route, one day ago" -- and the routes have no fixed size
      # to test against, which is the whole reason the rule above had to be absolute.
      #
      # ⚠️ THE THRESHOLD CLEARS THE PROGRAMME DROP. Polish cinemas publish a new week on Tuesday
      # afternoon and the showtime corpus roughly DOUBLES; page weight tracks showtimes, so a 2x
      # day-over-day rule would page every Tuesday and be turned off by the third week. 3x sits
      # above that and still well under the 7x and 12x measured above.
      - alert: WebResponseSizeJumped
        expr: >-
          (
            (
              sum by (country, route) (rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_sum{route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks).*"}[1h]))
              /
              sum by (country, route) (rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_count{route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks).*"}[1h]))
            )
            /
            (
              sum by (country, route) (rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_sum{route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks).*"}[1h] offset 1d))
              /
              sum by (country, route) (rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_count{route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks).*"}[1h] offset 1d))
            )
          ) > 3
          and on (country, route)
          (
            sum by (country, route) (
              rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_count{route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks).*"}[1h])
            ) >= 0.05
          )
          and on (country, route)
          (
            sum by (country, route) (
              rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_count{route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks).*"}[1h] offset 1d)
            ) >= 0.05
          )
        # Half an hour: a deploy that does this does it to every request immediately, so there is
        # nothing to wait out except a scrape or two. Long enough that a rollout's own cold cache
        # cannot trip it.
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} {{ $labels.route }} responses are {{ $value | humanize }}x yesterday's size"
          description: >-
            The mean response on this route is more than three times what it was a day ago. This is
            a step change rather than the corpus growing, so look at what deployed: the usual cause
            is content negotiation, where a change makes the origin answer one encoding while the
            edge caches another and serves some clients an unusable or decompressed body. Compare
            the origin against the edge for the SAME Accept-Encoding before anything else --
            `curl -H 'Accept-Encoding: gzip' -o /dev/null -w '%{size_download}'` against
            showtimes.cc, then the same with `--resolve` straight at the k3s node. A gap between
            them is the edge; no gap is the origin.

      # The mirror. A page that suddenly gets much SMALLER is the same class of accident and has
      # happened here: the 2026-08-10 read-model backfill pruned live screenings and the listings
      # went nearly empty, which no size ceiling can see. A third of yesterday is far below the
      # Tuesday-to-Monday decay of the programme drop.
      - alert: WebResponseSizeCollapsed
        expr: >-
          (
            (
              sum by (country, route) (rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_sum{route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks).*"}[1h]))
              /
              sum by (country, route) (rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_count{route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks).*"}[1h]))
            )
            /
            (
              sum by (country, route) (rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_sum{route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks).*"}[1h] offset 1d))
              /
              sum by (country, route) (rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_count{route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks).*"}[1h] offset 1d))
            )
          ) < 0.33
          and on (country, route)
          (
            sum by (country, route) (
              rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_count{route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks).*"}[1h])
            ) >= 0.05
          )
          and on (country, route)
          (
            sum by (country, route) (
              rate(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_count{route!~"other|.*(og-image|assets/|img-event|/uptime|/tasks).*"}[1h] offset 1d)
            ) >= 0.05
          )
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "{{ $labels.country }} {{ $labels.route }} responses are {{ $value | humanizePercentage }} of yesterday's size"
          description: >-
            The mean response on this route is under a third of a day ago. A listing does not
            shrink like that because the market did; it shrinks because it is rendering less than
            it should. Check the read model has screenings for the affected country before assuming
            the page template changed -- a prune that empties `web_screenings` renders a valid,
            fast, nearly empty page, and every other web alert stays green through it.

      # ⚠️ THE COMPANION. All three rules above can only fire on a series that exists, so a web tier
      # that stopped OBSERVING sizes would leave them permanently Normal -- indistinguishable from
      # pages that are the right size. `absent(kinowo_web_http_requests_total)` in web-errors.rules
      # does NOT cover this: sizes are recorded only when a response carries a Content-Length
      # (`WebHttpMetrics.responseLength`), so a change that made responses stream would keep the
      # request counter flowing while this histogram went silent.
      - alert: WebResponseSizeUnobserved
        expr: absent(kinowo_web_http_response_bytes_count)
        for: 30m
        labels:
          severity: warning
        annotations:
          summary: "No web response-size samples at all"
          description: >-
            Nothing in the web tier is reporting response sizes, so WebPageTooHeavy,
            WebResponseSizeJumped and WebResponseSizeCollapsed are all reading as healthy without
            being able to fire. Either the tier is down -- check web-errors.rules, which will say
            so -- or responses stopped carrying a Content-Length for the filter to measure.
